  "image_name": "d2f8dd30d59f0d335c8283b91fdeebaa.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a heat-to-work conversion concept based on a nanoporous membrane separating two liquid regions at different temperatures and pressures. A warm fluid at low pressure lies above the membrane and is heated by a heat source, while a cool fluid at high pressure lies below it and is connected to a heat sink. Thermometer and pressure icons emphasize the imposed gradient: high temperature/low pressure on top versus low temperature/high pressure below.\n\nThe enlarged view of the membrane suggests that vapor moves through nanoscale pores from the warm side to the cool side, indicated by downward arrows. This transport appears to be driven by the temperature difference while occurring against the pressure difference, from low pressure toward high pressure. The membrane therefore acts as a selective pathway for vapor flux between the two liquid phases.\n\nThe overall message is that thermal energy can be converted into mechanical work by sustaining vapor flow across the membrane. The equation at the bottom summarizes the idea: the generated work is proportional to the vapor flux multiplied by the pressure difference between the high- and low-pressure sides.",
